--- MESSAGE ---
A foreign derivation is spelled by its operator, and the root is appended (#51)

`Dictionary` opened `from Map(K, V) DracoType` while its own first element read
`fix kvMap {K, V}` — one concept spelled two ways in one file, three lines apart. This
makes the surface say it once: `from {K, V}`.

Three symptoms, one seam. The corpus census is what tied them together: across both
corpora, 130 definitions carrying 98 derivation references, exactly ONE reference carries
no package — `Dictionary`'s map parent. It is the same one reference behind all three.

  1. It was the last round-trip loss DrakeParseTest measured. A bare name in reference
     position means "the referring type's own package", so a package-less reference went
     out bare and came back owning `draco`.
  2. It is why the file spelled the map twice: the valueTypes went neutral in 0acf2da and
     the derivation could not follow, because a reference is nominal and an expression is
     not.
  3. It is why `Dictionary` was the only type in either corpus that spelled `DracoType`
     beside another parent. `TypeLoader.rooted` gated on `derivation.nonEmpty`, which
     cannot tell a parent that roots the type from one that does not, so the root had to
     be written by hand to compensate for a parent outside draco's graph.

WHAT CHANGED, and deliberately what did NOT.

The issue's own notes proposed making `derivation` hold type expressions — a shape change
on the most load-bearing type in the system. Rejected, and not on cost: 97 of the 98
references are package-qualified and structural, and `TypeName.namePackage` already models
exactly what a flattened expression string would give up. The issue text says as much
about `org.evrete.api.StatefulSession` in a valueType, one level down. A change that
regresses 97 references to fix 1 is not the progressive direction.

So the SPELLING changed, not the shape:

  - An empty `namePackage` now MEANS something rather than being an omission. Every draco
    type is declared inside a domain, so a reference with no package names a type outside
    draco's graph entirely — it is FOREIGN, a third case beside bare and qualified.
  - A foreign referent is spelled as a type expression: `{K, V}`. The operator carries
    what the name used to. Nothing is left to resolve, so the round trip is exact by
    construction rather than by normalization — which is the actual reason the loss closed.
  - `Drake.operatorCarried` states this off the emitter's own spelling instead of a second
    list of primitive names, so the two directions cannot drift apart.
  - `TypeLoader.rooted` APPENDS the root to a definition with no draco-domain parent,
    where it previously replaced the derivation of an empty one. For every type in the
    corpus but `Dictionary` that is the same operation it already performed.
  - Emission follows the same rule: the root is spelled only where loading would NOT put
    it back, so it stays off the surface alone and beside a foreign parent, and stays on
    beside a draco parent. That case is now unreachable from the corpus, which is the
    point — with `Dictionary` fixed, no definition names `DracoType`, in either notation.
  - One straggler went with it: `domains/world/Cartesian.json` still carried a sole
    `[DracoType]` derivation, which the 2026-07-29 strip (#50 item 1) did not reach because
    it covered the draco corpus only. Same argument as that commit — generation roots at
    the entry, so the output is unchanged — and it is what makes "no definition names the
    root" true of both corpora rather than one.

The normalization DrakeParseTest used to apply to `derivation`/`modules` packages is gone
(and the matching one in `DrakeCLI check`), so gate 2 verifies reference packages outright.
A gate that measures a loss and a gate that forbids it are different gates; this is the
second one now.

MEASURED BEFORE COMPILING. Both directions were simulated over the whole corpus first —
emit reproduces all 91 authored `.drake` headers with the new rule, and parse reconstructs
each of their JSON derivations including its package. That is the same method the two 2026-07-29
increments used, and the reason to trust the change before the suite confirms it.

WHAT REMAINS, honestly. A foreign type with no operator to spell it — a bare name outside
every draco domain — would still read as a same-package reference and come back wrong. The
corpus has none, and the loss report would name one if it arrived. Recorded in drake.dlt
under REFERENCES as the residual, in place of the one this closes.

This is the derivation edge of #51 only. Type expressions in `valueType` and the
`TypeParameter` atom are untouched and stay open on the issue.
